# api/web/whats-new.json is generated from CHANGELOG.md by scripts/whats-new/gen.mjs
# and committed, because ADR-0012 keeps the web UI buildless — nothing regenerates it
# at build or run time. CI regenerates it and fails on any diff.
#
# That combination has one sharp edge, and it has drawn blood three times: two branches
# that both add a changelog entry each regenerate the feed, and git then merges the two
# JSON files the way it merges any text — hunk by hunk, with no idea that the result has
# to be a *function* of the merged CHANGELOG. Nothing conflicts. The merge lands, the
# feed is silently wrong (an entry duplicated, or one past the generator's cap left in),
# and main goes red on its own push rather than on either branch — where the failure
# reads as unrelated to whatever merged last.
#
# `-merge` takes git out of that business: a generated file is not mergeable, so when
# both sides changed it git raises a conflict instead of inventing an answer. The
# resolution is never to pick a side — it is to take the merged CHANGELOG.md and run
# `make whats-new` (or `node scripts/whats-new/gen.mjs`), which is what CI checks anyway.
#
# This covers merges *git* performs — a branch updated locally, or a rebase. It does not
# cover GitHub's merge button, which ignores the attribute: the merge of #555 conflicts
# here and landed clean there. The backstop for that path is
# .github/workflows/whats-new-sync.yml, which regenerates the feed on main and pushes the
# correction. Preventing the bad merge outright takes branch protection's "require
# branches to be up to date before merging", which is a repository setting, not a file.
api/web/whats-new.json -merge
